1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Domed roofs, the most organic space saving way to cover a stadium.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:12,000
In cities with hostile weather, they keep the big game excitement in and the unwanted elements out.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Retractable roofs, the cutting edge in stadium design.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
They slide on rails, roll on wheels, or move on pulleys.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:27,000
These state of the art coverings allow teams to adopt to the climate in a matter of minutes,

6
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,720
making weather delays a thing of the past.

7
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:33,800
Now, Dome Stadiums on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:49,460
Because of cold weather and winter storms,

9
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,980
baseball was only played in summer.

10
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:55,440
But even then, searing heat and sudden showers

11
00:00:55,441 --> 00:00:57,100
could quickly cut games short.

12
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:02,260
Likewise, other spectator sports, like football,

13
00:01:02,261 --> 00:01:03,820
could be seasonally disadvantaged.

14
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:07,260
And the cost of building and maintaining large stadiums

15
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:09,740
demanded year-round use.

16
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,920
It was a very practical notion to consider

17
00:01:13,921 --> 00:01:16,240
an artificial environment to house big games

18
00:01:16,241 --> 00:01:16,941
from the elements.

19
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,340
And it was a very American notion

20
00:01:19,341 --> 00:01:21,460
to accept the incredible scale

21
00:01:21,461 --> 00:01:23,840
which would be demanded in such a project.

22
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:30,520
A dimension achieved in such accomplishments as Boulder Dam and the skyscrapers of Manhattan.

23
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:36,020
The challenge of a structure's purpose often defines its design.

24
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:39,780
As architect Louis Sullivan noted, form follows function.

25
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:48,180
In putting a roof over a stadium, there would be no place to position support columns without disrupting the action of a game.

26
00:01:48,181 --> 00:01:56,420
The answer is an arched roof, which needs no columns because its weight is diverted laterally and supported by the building's walls.

27
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:03,980
Those walls then need buttresses to keep from buckling under the increased load and further dissipate the overall weight.

28
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:07,160
This is not a new idea.

29
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:14,940
The concept has been used for centuries in the construction of Gothic cathedrals, where great height could not be accommodated through the support of columns.

30
00:02:14,941 --> 00:02:21,940
The modern approach, however, recalls earlier structures based on a dome design.

31
00:02:21,941 --> 00:02:27,940
In this situation, the ceiling and the walls were a single unit, and the entire weight

32
00:02:27,941 --> 00:02:33,940
of the building flows evenly to the ground, equally distributed over its 360 degrees.

33
00:02:33,941 --> 00:02:40,940
Likewise, the gravity of the sides meets in the middle, making the center support unnecessary.

34
00:02:40,941 --> 00:02:48,680
unnecessary. The shape also results in energy conservation as thermal waves are

35
00:02:48,681 --> 00:02:53,440
both reflected and circulated by the curved interior surface. The antecedents

36
00:02:53,441 --> 00:02:58,620
of the dome is the fact that it's the simplest way of covering the space if

37
00:02:58,621 --> 00:03:05,700
it's not too big and you have the Inuit ice house and the notion of shelter used

38
00:03:05,701 --> 00:03:10,700
in buildings made of vegetation, made of wood, sod.

39
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,000
As the appeal of modern team sports continued to grow,

40
00:03:15,001 --> 00:03:16,700
so did the need for domed stadiums

41
00:03:16,701 --> 00:03:19,200
to facilitate year-round events.

42
00:03:19,201 --> 00:03:22,360
The only missing ingredient was money,

43
00:03:22,361 --> 00:03:23,760
but America had plenty of that

44
00:03:23,761 --> 00:03:27,340
in the second half of the 1900s, especially in Texas.

45
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,940
It had been a flat, barren frontier

46
00:03:31,941 --> 00:03:34,900
where grazing herds of steer roamed free.

47
00:03:34,901 --> 00:03:40,460
Then vast amounts of oil were discovered underground in the 1930s, and the landscape changed.

48
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:49,820
Cattlemen, gunslingers, and outlaws were replaced by a more dangerous breed, oilmen, accountants, and lawyers.

49
00:03:52,180 --> 00:04:01,000
By the 1960s, sports fans in hot and humid Houston were demanding an indoor air-conditioned arena, and the Astrodome was born.

50
00:04:01,001 --> 00:04:06,000
The Astrodome was state-of-the-art when it became the very first dome stadium.

51
00:04:06,001 --> 00:04:14,000
But the self-proclaimed eighth wonder of the world had not worked out all the bugs before it opened in 1965.

52
00:04:14,001 --> 00:04:19,000
The first controversy was the giant facility's air conditioning system.

53
00:04:19,001 --> 00:04:26,000
During its inaugural season, the visiting New York Mets claimed that the Astros were using the dome's cooling system to create wind currents,

54
00:04:26,001 --> 00:04:30,000
which helped lift and carry the home team's long balls.

55
00:04:30,001 --> 00:04:36,640
Another problem arose from the thousands of giant glass panels in the ceiling.

56
00:04:36,641 --> 00:04:41,760
The problem was that when outfielders were going to field fly balls, they lost them in

57
00:04:41,761 --> 00:04:45,780
the glare when they would look up at these bright panels.

58
00:04:45,781 --> 00:04:51,080
So the team experimented with covering several of the panels so that outfielders would be

59
00:04:51,081 --> 00:04:52,080
able to see.

60
00:04:52,081 --> 00:04:56,800
But when they did that, there wasn't enough sunlight to let the grass keep growing.

61
00:04:56,801 --> 00:05:00,300
So modern technology came along with AstroTurf.

62
00:05:00,301 --> 00:05:03,440
So you saw the advent of artificial turf

63
00:05:03,441 --> 00:05:06,640
with the Astrodome in Houston.

64
00:05:06,641 --> 00:05:07,520
There are drawbacks.

65
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:09,180
Physically, I think it beats up players

66
00:05:09,181 --> 00:05:11,640
when they have to play on it on a daily basis.

67
00:05:11,641 --> 00:05:15,320
Underneath this artificial surface is concrete.

68
00:05:15,321 --> 00:05:16,180
There's no padding.

69
00:05:16,181 --> 00:05:17,880
It's built into the turf itself.

70
00:05:17,881 --> 00:05:22,320
So it is hard, and it does pound on you.

71
00:05:22,321 --> 00:05:25,000
Players had problems with the Astrodome surface,

72
00:05:25,001 --> 00:05:26,320
but this was a minor criticism

73
00:05:26,321 --> 00:05:29,520
when compared to the reception of Seattle's Kingdome.

74
00:05:29,521 --> 00:05:32,560
The cavernous stadium was nicknamed the Tomb

75
00:05:32,561 --> 00:05:35,300
for its gray lifeless interior.

76
00:05:35,301 --> 00:05:37,300
In addition to its dull appearance,

77
00:05:37,301 --> 00:05:38,600
there were concerns that the building

78
00:05:38,601 --> 00:05:40,740
was not structurally sound.

79
00:05:40,741 --> 00:05:43,940
The Kingdome, there was one incident in the mid 90s

80
00:05:43,941 --> 00:05:47,040
where ceiling tiles fell from the ceiling.

81
00:05:47,041 --> 00:05:49,000
The process of taking down the tiles

82
00:05:49,001 --> 00:05:51,680
and blasting away the glue spots left behind

83
00:05:51,681 --> 00:05:53,640
is a serious complication.

84
00:05:53,641 --> 00:05:59,860
I heard the broadcaster for the Seattle Mariners say one time that even though everyone seemed to hate the King Dome, he loved it.

85
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,240
Because that's what allowed Major League Baseball to come to Seattle.

86
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:09,740
Without that dome, there would have been rain out after rain out, and Major League Baseball probably would not have gone in that direction.

87
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:15,860
Still, a major stadium can be an attractive device to pump new life into a depressed city.

88
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:21,300
That was the case in New Orleans in 1971, when work began on the Superdome.

89
00:06:21,301 --> 00:06:30,440
Stretching over 52 acres of old train yards, it was designed to boost tourism by attracting major sporting, entertainment, and political events.

90
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:35,340
The Superdome featured the engineering marvel of a domed roof as its hook.

91
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:46,326
20,000 tons of steel raised the dome 273 feet over the playing field, so great a height that if the Statue of Liberty made her home in the outfield, her torch would

92
00:06:46,327 --> 00:06:47,820
just scrape the ceiling.

93
00:06:47,821 --> 00:06:50,920
The building is enormous inside.

94
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:55,400
Six stupendous television screens are suspended from a center overhead gondola

95
00:06:55,401 --> 00:06:57,860
to allow spectators a good view of the action.

96
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:03,480
Cost overruns and inflation helped bring the final price tag of the Superdome

97
00:07:03,481 --> 00:07:05,720
to $163 million.

98
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:10,260
It succeeded in amazing the locals and bringing in tourists,

99
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:15,360
but its size and cost overruns resulted in a $5.5 million operating loss

100
00:07:15,361 --> 00:07:17,460
in each of its first 10 years.

101
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:25,120
In 2005, the Superdome served a far more vital role to the city of New Orleans, when thousands

102
00:07:25,121 --> 00:07:29,560
took refuge there after Hurricane Katrina devastated the city.

103
00:07:29,561 --> 00:07:37,860
Montreal, 1976, home of the 21st Olympic Games, and a whimsical approach to a retractable

104
00:07:37,861 --> 00:07:44,060
roof stadium in an environment plagued by harsh winter storms.

105
00:07:44,061 --> 00:07:48,240
Here is a structure that is only partially domed, the balance of the task performed by

106
00:07:48,241 --> 00:07:50,440
a giant umbrella.

107
00:07:50,441 --> 00:07:57,360
26 cables drape a 50-ton cover over the stadium from the tallest inclined tower in the world,

108
00:07:57,361 --> 00:07:59,900
over 50 stories high.

109
00:07:59,901 --> 00:08:05,500
In 1992, this tent covering collapsed under tons of accumulated snow.

110
00:08:05,501 --> 00:08:09,400
The French architect who designed the facility had been unfamiliar with Montreal's winter

111
00:08:09,401 --> 00:08:15,800
environment and hadn't added the weight into his equations.

112
00:08:15,801 --> 00:08:21,960
Soon after the accident, the administration of Montreal Mayor Drapeau also collapsed,

113
00:08:21,961 --> 00:08:26,120
a casualty of the calamity.

114
00:08:26,121 --> 00:08:30,800
Considering the mounting costs and concerns, it seemed unlikely that anyone would completely

115
00:08:30,801 --> 00:08:34,280
succeed in the effort to build a dome stadium.

116
00:08:34,281 --> 00:08:39,280
But in 1987, the city of Toronto embarked on a bold new approach.

117
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:47,280
Here, simplicity and ingenuity would join hands and inspire the ultimate arena.

118
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:56,280
Only one baseball player ever hit the roof of the Astrodome during a game.

119
00:08:57,280 --> 00:09:02,280
Mike Schmidt of the Philadelphia Phillies on June 10, 1974.

120
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:11,180
I've always said it wasn't the worst stadium in baseball.

121
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:12,660
It was the worst stadium in sports.

122
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:14,900
What they did was take a bad football stadium,

123
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:16,900
make it a very bad baseball stadium.

124
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:21,060
Exhibition Stadium was the official name

125
00:09:21,061 --> 00:09:22,640
for Toronto's Major League Arena.

126
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:26,560
Most fans referred to it as the mistake by the lake.

127
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:30,180
It was a retrofit of an old football stadium.

128
00:09:30,181 --> 00:09:33,240
We built it in the wrong location.

129
00:09:33,241 --> 00:09:37,980
We knew that the traffic patterns were awful, the sight lines were awful, but at least it

130
00:09:37,981 --> 00:09:43,120
fitted the bill temporarily before we could build what we really wanted to build.

131
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:48,960
In 1986, the city of Toronto was ready to commit itself to an ambitious new stadium,

132
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:55,960
which would invigorate the downtown area, excite sports fans, and propel the city into the future.

133
00:09:57,180 --> 00:10:01,340
We thought that if we could do something that Houston was able to accomplish,

134
00:10:01,341 --> 00:10:05,020
or the city of Seattle itself, we'd be going a long way.

135
00:10:05,220 --> 00:10:08,020
As we started down the road of a dome stadium,

136
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:13,480
it became apparent that a lot of the baseball players prefer to play outdoors.

137
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:17,220
In fact, Buck Martinez, one of the Blue Jay catchers at the time,

138
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:19,320
took me off into the corner and said,

139
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:22,200
Congratulations on getting a stadium through. I think that's great.

140
00:10:22,201 --> 00:10:26,500
But from a player's standpoint, I don't think players are going to like playing indoors.

141
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:30,980
Well, I guess I was sort of horrified by the fact that here I'd been campaigning

142
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:34,100
since 1969 to build a dome stadium.

143
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:36,700
Here we are getting ready and finally in the 80s

144
00:10:36,701 --> 00:10:38,180
to build a stadium.

145
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:40,420
And I was finally learning for the first time

146
00:10:40,421 --> 00:10:43,040
that maybe the players didn't want to play inside.

147
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:46,180
Then I suggested a retractable roof.

148
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:47,920
I said, boy, that would be the best of both worlds

149
00:10:47,921 --> 00:10:49,640
because you have the weather problem in Toronto.

150
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:52,480
You would alleviate all of those concerns.

151
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:54,740
You'd be able to play in perfect conditions all the time.

152
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:56,660
But when it was nice in Toronto, you could open the door

153
00:10:56,661 --> 00:10:59,300
and you would have a convertible stadium.

154
00:10:59,301 --> 00:11:02,300
So we quickly went back to the drawing board and said,

155
00:11:02,301 --> 00:11:04,300
look, if we're going to do something in Toronto,

156
00:11:04,301 --> 00:11:06,300
we should be doing something really unique.

157
00:11:06,301 --> 00:11:14,300
In March of 83, the government announced that it was going to look into building a covered stadium

158
00:11:14,301 --> 00:11:17,300
for the Toronto area.

159
00:11:17,301 --> 00:11:23,300
And I went after the mayor of Toronto, Mr. Eggleton, who was then mayor,

160
00:11:23,301 --> 00:11:27,300
asked him for the job and he said, you and 40,000 others.

161
00:11:27,301 --> 00:11:35,300
Robby joined forces with his old friend Michael Allen, an engineer with the ingenuity to develop a new kind of kinetic building.

162
00:11:35,301 --> 00:11:43,300
Basically the question that Rod put to us was, do you want to waste a lot of time, a lot of money trying to go after a dream?

163
00:11:43,301 --> 00:11:47,300
And we said, well why not? This is a wonderful idea, let's go after it.

164
00:11:47,301 --> 00:11:52,300
And we originally treated this as, in essence, an academic exercise.

165
00:11:52,301 --> 00:11:56,648
academic exercise. Let's see if we can solve the basic problem.

166
00:11:56,649 --> 00:11:58,580
How do you get a retractable

167
00:11:58,581 --> 00:12:05,360
roof to work for a dome structure and have the roof, when it is retracted, remain on

168
00:12:05,361 --> 00:12:08,403
the building footprint and not parked on someone else's property?

169
00:12:08,404 --> 00:12:09,760
And we said, what a wonderful

170
00:12:09,761 --> 00:12:12,718
thing to get into and see if we can solve that problem.

171
00:12:12,719 --> 00:12:14,600
It was a case of David and Goliath,

172
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:19,680
with Robbie and Alan putting up their homes to raise the capital to design a proposal

173
00:12:19,681 --> 00:12:23,520
which would rival their well-funded competition.

174
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:26,120
But this was a challenge that demanded more imagination

175
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:27,420
than money.

176
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:30,320
It began with sketches that suggested a general aesthetic

177
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:34,320
theme, a curved arching form reminiscent of classic domed

178
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:35,720
structures.

179
00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:40,440
But this timeless approach only solved half the equation.

180
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:44,140
The dome by itself was not enough.

181
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:48,440
And this particularly came from the baseball crowd who

182
00:12:48,441 --> 00:12:51,860
who insisted they wanted to be able to watch baseball

183
00:12:51,861 --> 00:12:54,440
in an open-air stadium when the weather is nice.

184
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:56,720
You want to sit out and enjoy the weather,

185
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:58,040
have a beer, and enjoy the game.

186
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:02,320
Two of these panels move in a linear motion,

187
00:13:03,060 --> 00:13:05,920
and one of the panels moves on a circular track.

188
00:13:06,740 --> 00:13:09,760
And when the retraction is completed,

189
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:13,380
all of the panels stack one top of the other

190
00:13:13,381 --> 00:13:15,260
in a telescoping form.

191
00:13:15,261 --> 00:13:20,260
From the computer model, a miniature was assembled.

192
00:13:20,261 --> 00:13:23,260
Just like early mud huts and igloos,

193
00:13:23,261 --> 00:13:27,260
here was a structurally sound unit.

194
00:13:27,261 --> 00:13:30,260
The basic stress flows like water down a stream.

195
00:13:30,261 --> 00:13:35,260
It is an arch, and the load flows down through the arch

196
00:13:35,261 --> 00:13:38,260
to the supports and into the concrete structure.

197
00:13:38,261 --> 00:13:43,260
This roof span is over 700 feet,

198
00:13:43,261 --> 00:13:48,040
and the roof structure is about 15 feet in depth.

199
00:13:48,041 --> 00:13:52,760
The roof is designed to support about 600 tons.

200
00:13:53,300 --> 00:13:56,180
We could support 30 DC-9s from the roof,

201
00:13:56,560 --> 00:13:58,000
providing they could all fit in there.

202
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:02,980
The roof opens so that 91% of the seats

203
00:14:02,981 --> 00:14:05,420
in virtually all of the field is open to the sky.

204
00:14:06,140 --> 00:14:07,820
There were concerns that every fan

205
00:14:07,821 --> 00:14:10,660
wouldn't enjoy a direct view of all the action in a game.

206
00:14:10,661 --> 00:14:14,360
The fans wanted to be as close as possible to the players

207
00:14:14,361 --> 00:14:19,760
and vice versa, and that the stadium should have an intimacy.

208
00:14:21,180 --> 00:14:25,360
Once again, computer models simulated the sight lines from every seat.

209
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:29,980
And once again, the designers employed a practical test as well.

210
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,260
They had a stadium with a roof on it,

211
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:38,100
and then they would take a lamp around to act like the sun,

212
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:40,240
and they'd watch the shadows inside the stadium.

213
00:14:40,241 --> 00:14:42,840
So that's how the process developed.

214
00:14:43,240 --> 00:14:46,540
It had to have a front and a back, and it had to face the sun.

215
00:14:46,700 --> 00:14:51,480
It was going to open up to let the sun come in, so it should retract to the north.

216
00:14:51,980 --> 00:15:00,300
And when you saw it, its movements should be complex, elegant, and intriguing.

217
00:15:01,660 --> 00:15:04,220
Robbie and Alan won the construction contract.

218
00:15:04,221 --> 00:15:10,580
And now it was up to the stadium management to fund the $210 million estimated cost.

219
00:15:11,140 --> 00:15:14,800
The Metropolitan Toronto put up $30 million towards the stadium.

220
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:18,540
The Ontario government also put up another $30 million.

221
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:28,800
The balance was put up $150 million at that time by 30 private sector companies putting up $5 million each.

222
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:33,800
Ironically, as groundbreaking ceremonies commenced on June 1, 1987,

223
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:41,220
Robbie had to admit that he was less than familiar with the games which would be played in the futuristic dome.

224
00:15:41,221 --> 00:15:46,220
When I got involved in Skydome, I'd never been to a baseball game.

225
00:15:46,221 --> 00:15:50,220
I learned what baseball was as if it was nuclear physics.

226
00:15:50,221 --> 00:15:56,220
Instead, Robbie broadened the attitude of the project and gave it a deeper meaning.

227
00:15:56,221 --> 00:16:03,220
It seemed to me that this building was going to be more than just a sports park.

228
00:16:03,221 --> 00:16:07,300
It had a multi, it was a multifunctional building.

229
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:16,000
And I coined the word secular cathedral simply to say it was very big and it belonged to everybody.

230
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:22,020
The first World Series game ever played outside of the United States was played at the Toronto Skydome.

231
00:16:26,580 --> 00:16:32,940
The Blue Jays had to open on a given date, at a given time, and there was no forgiveness.

232
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:34,140
It had to do it.

233
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:40,680
From the very beginning, project management focused on building team spirit first and a dome second.

234
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:48,000
A 360-degree club was initiated, with hats that kept the broad vision of the endeavor on everyone's mind.

235
00:16:49,620 --> 00:16:52,560
A unity of purpose quickly spread through the team.

236
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:58,920
Ellis Don had a constant crew of about 650 on the site.

237
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:02,680
They worked 20 hours a day, six days a week.

238
00:17:02,681 --> 00:17:07,160
At the end, we had about 2,200 people working on the job

239
00:17:07,161 --> 00:17:09,780
to bring it to its final completion.

240
00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:14,440
We figured that there were around 5,000 people involved

241
00:17:14,441 --> 00:17:15,300
in this building.

242
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:19,200
The intense effort to meet completion deadlines

243
00:17:19,201 --> 00:17:22,160
rivaled the extraordinary scope of the undertaking.

244
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:26,300
The dome was designed to be taller than a 31-story building,

245
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:29,560
large enough to house eight 747s,

246
00:17:29,561 --> 00:17:35,280
a herd of 751 elephants, or a 32-home suburban community.

247
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:41,100
Even the original Roman Coliseum could easily fit on the playing field.

248
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:44,220
It was 24 hours a day.

249
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:50,080
We did all the concrete off-site and brought it in at nighttime and assembled it in the stadium.

250
00:17:50,940 --> 00:17:55,640
The basic structure was composed of over 210,000 tons of concrete

251
00:17:55,641 --> 00:17:58,700
and 15,000 tons of reinforcing steel.

252
00:17:59,560 --> 00:18:07,140
Many sections were prefabricated and trucked in after dark when empty roads could accommodate an endless parade of oversized trucks.

253
00:18:09,220 --> 00:18:18,720
It was important to work at a frantic pace because everyone realized that Toronto's harsh climate could slow the effort to an agonizing crawl.

254
00:18:18,721 --> 00:18:32,100
It was Toronto's bitterly cold winters that took the greatest toll on construction and on the fortitude of the workers.

255
00:18:32,820 --> 00:18:37,340
Many times their fingers were so numb, they couldn't feel the tools in their hands.

256
00:18:37,341 --> 00:18:51,341
8 hours a day, or 10, or 12, the brutal elements made progress a painful victory.

257
00:18:52,880 --> 00:18:57,780
The steel shell of the dome covered with thin white plastic sheeting would eventually trap

258
00:18:57,781 --> 00:19:02,560
the warmth of the stadium inside for the comfort of the fans, and the structure could stand under

259
00:19:02,561 --> 00:19:05,760
the strain of unlimited accumulations of snow on top.

260
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:17,260
More than 12,000 person years of work went into the effort, from laying foundations to

261
00:19:17,261 --> 00:19:25,440
hand-pounding massive support girders into alignment, to installing 1,280 toilets, 9,000

262
00:19:25,441 --> 00:19:30,320
light bulbs, and 67,000 spectator seats.

263
00:19:30,321 --> 00:19:36,060
The roof alone required a quarter million bolts, but even that couldn't completely reinforce

264
00:19:36,061 --> 00:19:37,260
the public's confidence.

265
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:40,220
Not only did the roof have to work safely,

266
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:43,760
but it had to be perceived to be working safely

267
00:19:43,761 --> 00:19:46,720
so that people would have no concern at all

268
00:19:46,721 --> 00:19:50,760
when 8,000 tons of steel is rotating and moving around

269
00:19:50,761 --> 00:19:52,400
and the game carries on.

270
00:19:52,660 --> 00:19:56,820
We had reports in the papers from so-called soothsayers.

271
00:19:56,920 --> 00:19:58,840
We had one very important gentleman

272
00:19:58,841 --> 00:20:02,620
who had predicted Kennedy's death and a few other events,

273
00:20:02,621 --> 00:20:08,380
and he made headlines predicting the roof would never open, it would never work.

274
00:20:10,840 --> 00:20:17,280
The most emotional experience was the day we closed the roof, we built the roof open

275
00:20:17,281 --> 00:20:20,880
and then we moved panel one to the south end of the building.

276
00:20:20,881 --> 00:20:28,920
It moved very slowly and we were all up there and touching the roof as it moved for the first time.

277
00:20:28,921 --> 00:20:38,200
The first day I moved and I had a chance, the opportunity to ride shotgun as she turned

278
00:20:38,201 --> 00:20:40,560
from north to south.

279
00:20:41,340 --> 00:20:42,760
It was a super day.

280
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:43,740
It was a super day.

281
00:20:44,320 --> 00:20:49,800
It's not every day you see a structure that's 500 feet start to emerge.

282
00:20:49,801 --> 00:20:53,880
It was like being born from the rest of the structure as it came out.

283
00:20:54,320 --> 00:20:55,340
Grown men were crying.

284
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:57,920
I mean, this was a very magical moment.

285
00:20:57,921 --> 00:21:01,680
It was a very, very touching, moving experience

286
00:21:01,681 --> 00:21:05,740
because there were so many people had said it wouldn't work,

287
00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:09,200
and this was the first manifestation that it would indeed work.

288
00:21:09,880 --> 00:21:11,880
Ironically, work was nearly complete

289
00:21:11,881 --> 00:21:16,460
when attention finally turned to an important missing element.

290
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:19,180
We ran a great contest naming Sky Dome here, too.

291
00:21:19,300 --> 00:21:21,300
We had 150,000 entries.

292
00:21:21,820 --> 00:21:25,100
We had 12,000 different names, and we had 2,000 Sky Domes.

293
00:21:25,101 --> 00:21:34,780
For us, it was either always the dome or the condom, because when we were designing the building, of course, it didn't have a name.

294
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:41,480
We suggested condom to our client, and there was a lot of chuckling.

295
00:21:41,960 --> 00:21:45,780
So we narrowed it down with a group of 16 judges to the word Skydome.

296
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:50,020
We had the premier of the day reach into a drum, pulling out a name.

297
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:52,160
Now, the prize was two tickets for life.

298
00:21:52,161 --> 00:21:55,920
I leaned over to the premier and said, I hope you pick somebody really old.

299
00:21:57,240 --> 00:22:01,700
The winner was Kelly Watson, young school teacher from Ontario,

300
00:22:02,040 --> 00:22:06,840
who now enjoys lifetime seats valued at over $10,000 a year.

301
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:10,640
But this big prize was only part of the cost overrun.

302
00:22:11,360 --> 00:22:15,220
The stadium didn't come in on budget because we kept adding things to it.

303
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:17,840
We added the hotel, which was $110 million.

304
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:19,760
We added a health club.

305
00:22:19,761 --> 00:22:22,760
Our initial boxes were only going to be 90.

306
00:22:22,761 --> 00:22:24,760
The demand said we should add more of those,

307
00:22:24,761 --> 00:22:26,760
so we got up to 161.

308
00:22:26,761 --> 00:22:30,760
The total cost of the project was 570 million.

309
00:22:30,761 --> 00:22:32,760
Like a massive mechanical flower,

310
00:22:32,761 --> 00:22:35,760
SkyDome blossomed into a multipurpose center

311
00:22:35,761 --> 00:22:38,760
designed to service the world's biggest crowds.

312
00:22:38,761 --> 00:22:41,760
It included 25 fast food outlets

313
00:22:41,761 --> 00:22:45,760
and the largest McDonald's restaurant in North America.

314
00:22:45,761 --> 00:22:53,760
A 350-room luxury hotel is part of the structure, as well as a movie theater and a $13 million

315
00:22:53,761 --> 00:22:54,760
television studio.

316
00:22:54,761 --> 00:23:01,940
Three years and eight months to do everything in a building that went from being just a

317
00:23:01,941 --> 00:23:07,760
stadium into a multi-use facility with a hotel in it and a lot of other things.

318
00:23:07,761 --> 00:23:13,760
Considering the size of this job, there were very, very few accidents during the construction

319
00:23:13,761 --> 00:23:14,760
of the building.

320
00:23:14,761 --> 00:23:19,500
Unfortunately, some fatalities after it was finished on some clean-up work.

321
00:23:19,620 --> 00:23:23,260
There were two accidents, both of which killed two people.

322
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:27,080
Under the spinning and reticulating ceiling of Skydome,

323
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:31,520
the energy and dreams of a city has been focused on one spectacular goal.

324
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:36,000
But as opening day approached, everyone was secretly wondering,

325
00:23:36,740 --> 00:23:38,800
would the performance match the promise?

326
00:23:40,280 --> 00:23:43,840
The Skydome was renamed the Rogers Center in 2005

327
00:23:43,841 --> 00:23:46,740
when the stadium was purchased by Rogers Communications.

328
00:23:52,320 --> 00:23:54,100
June 3, 1989.

329
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:58,780
Toronto gathered for a first look at their new half-billion-dollar stadium

330
00:23:58,781 --> 00:24:02,860
with a grand opening celebration as large as the stadium itself.

331
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:08,360
In an explosion of light and sound, Skydome was born.

332
00:24:10,220 --> 00:24:13,520
As part of the opening ceremonies, all the workers came in

333
00:24:13,521 --> 00:24:20,260
and paraded through the stadium and received a standing ovation from the crowd that was here.

334
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:28,260
The iron workers, the electricians, the designers and plumbers were joined by over 5,000 performers,

335
00:24:28,920 --> 00:24:32,260
including a chorus line of 750 dancers.

336
00:24:33,220 --> 00:24:38,660
A joyous sea of humanity covered the field to usher in a new era in stadium spectaculars

337
00:24:38,661 --> 00:24:41,800
and see the dome open for the very first time.

338
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:50,000
Four, three, two, one, yeah!

339
00:25:07,723 --> 00:25:13,620
The crowd was so anxious for the experience, no one complained that a storm passing over

340
00:25:13,621 --> 00:25:18,360
the facility actually caused everyone to be drenched in a warm summer rain.

341
00:25:18,361 --> 00:25:21,320
If everyone got wet, it could have happened to be raining at the time.

342
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:23,200
But what a wonderful event.

343
00:25:23,640 --> 00:25:26,820
I think the media would have been really critical had we not opened the roof.

344
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:30,080
We also had six parachute jumpers outside,

345
00:25:30,140 --> 00:25:32,640
and that would have been very difficult for them if we hadn't opened it.

346
00:25:32,980 --> 00:25:34,160
So we did open it.

347
00:25:34,680 --> 00:25:36,860
When that roof opened up for the first time,

348
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:38,480
and really in the middle of a rainstorm,

349
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:40,940
my goosebumps got goosebumps.

350
00:25:41,740 --> 00:25:43,200
There was a tear in my eye,

351
00:25:43,201 --> 00:25:53,660
And to this day, I will never forget the emotion of trying to explain to my three sons how the Sky Dome was built.

352
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:58,680
And they were watching a bit of Canadian history and Toronto history really take place.

353
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:03,840
Opening night of the Sky Dome was more than a celebration of a new era of sports in Toronto.

354
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:10,260
It was an opportunity for the city to salute the men and women of the community who made that vision a reality.

355
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:21,700
I think in about 1988 there was a national competition that I read about to design a

356
00:26:21,701 --> 00:26:24,440
workers recognition mural for the Skydome.

357
00:26:24,441 --> 00:26:30,200
And it was the first time that I'd seen a call for entry for a piece that was going to

358
00:26:30,201 --> 00:26:31,960
recognize workers.

359
00:26:31,961 --> 00:26:38,000
The need and desire of the money men who built the stadium to recognize the men and women

360
00:26:38,001 --> 00:26:39,000
who built it.

361
00:26:39,001 --> 00:26:43,980
And I think that it's always been very important for workers to say, I built this.

362
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:48,180
And here, they have the opportunity, their signatures are right on the piece.

363
00:26:48,880 --> 00:26:51,600
I have 1,800 names in this piece.

364
00:26:52,120 --> 00:27:02,940
We discovered a method, a photoemotion method, of actually getting their signatures and transferring them onto glass and sandblasting it into black flash glass.

365
00:27:02,941 --> 00:27:08,580
I think the day that this Skydom opened and they came up and they looked for their name was a very exciting moment.

366
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:17,020
Two nights later, it was the Blue Jays' turn to take flight in the new stadium, although

367
00:27:17,021 --> 00:27:19,920
preparations for the first pitch were far from complete.

368
00:27:19,921 --> 00:27:25,000
We were very excited about being the team to open the Sky Dome because it had such a

369
00:27:25,001 --> 00:27:29,620
buildup in terms of being the first convertible dome, so to speak.

370
00:27:29,621 --> 00:27:33,720
And I'll always remember coming out to the ballpark that day about 1.30, walking out

371
00:27:33,721 --> 00:27:35,760
here onto the field.

372
00:27:35,761 --> 00:27:36,760
The tarp wasn't down.

373
00:27:36,761 --> 00:27:38,000
There was no dirt on the field.

374
00:27:38,001 --> 00:27:41,460
The fences weren't up, and we said, there's no way we're going to be able to play this

375
00:27:41,461 --> 00:27:42,161
game tonight.

376
00:27:42,520 --> 00:27:45,540
And sure enough, about 4.30 came around, and they were making progress.

377
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:48,900
They said, hey, batting practice is canceled, but we still think we can start the game on

378
00:27:48,901 --> 00:27:49,601
time.

379
00:27:49,640 --> 00:27:53,120
About 6.30, they said, well, there's not going to be infield practice, but we still think

380
00:27:53,121 --> 00:27:54,320
we can start the game on time.

381
00:27:54,780 --> 00:27:58,800
About 7.15, they're putting down the dirt at home plate, putting in home plate.

382
00:27:59,260 --> 00:28:01,280
The fences are just being completed, putting up.

383
00:28:01,281 --> 00:28:04,060
And sure enough, at 7.35, the first pitch is thrown.

384
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:12,260
I happen to be the first batter facing Jimmy Key.

385
00:28:12,840 --> 00:28:16,360
Didn't swing at the first pitch because there was way too many flashbulbs going off.

386
00:28:16,500 --> 00:28:18,580
I had trouble discerning the ball from the light,

387
00:28:19,160 --> 00:28:21,760
and eventually, not a bat, hit a double to left center field.

388
00:28:22,060 --> 00:28:25,300
So a small part of Skydome history,

389
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:28,720
but one that I can say that I'm a part of forever, really.

390
00:28:32,500 --> 00:28:35,460
Great joy can come from the most humble of desires.

391
00:28:35,461 --> 00:28:40,080
to sit in the stands with a cold drink and a hot dog,

392
00:28:41,340 --> 00:28:44,020
to cheer the boys of summer as they run the bases,

393
00:28:45,340 --> 00:28:49,680
to glance up at the open sky and feel the fresh breeze.

394
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:13,560
The pitcher's mound, which is interesting, is actually a big fiberglass tub floating

395
00:29:13,561 --> 00:29:15,680
on some water.

396
00:29:15,681 --> 00:29:20,400
And if we're going to go to football, we let the water out underneath this tub and the

397
00:29:20,401 --> 00:29:24,020
whole mound disappears down to the ground and then we put a cover over it.

398
00:29:24,021 --> 00:29:29,820
The design of the Sky Dome held many secrets, but the biggest mysteries were buried at the

399
00:29:29,821 --> 00:29:34,540
site and uncovered before the first bulldozers plowed the soil.

400
00:29:34,541 --> 00:29:37,440
told us that this was really the site of a huge garbage dump.

401
00:29:37,441 --> 00:29:41,140
There's probably a better way to put that, but that's really what it was.

402
00:29:41,141 --> 00:29:43,180
In the old days, they were trying to reclaim the waterfront.

403
00:29:43,181 --> 00:29:44,840
Of course, they needed real estate.

404
00:29:44,841 --> 00:29:49,020
And one way to do that was to dump anything, virtually anything that wasn't still alive,

405
00:29:49,021 --> 00:29:51,520
they would dump into the water on the water's edge.

406
00:29:51,521 --> 00:29:56,720
And so what we have here is a time capsule of what people were throwing out back in the,

407
00:29:56,721 --> 00:29:59,660
well, as early as 1850, 1860.

408
00:29:59,661 --> 00:30:03,880
Building permits from the city of Toronto demanded that the landfill site, which would

409
00:30:03,881 --> 00:30:08,880
that support the Sky Dome be studied for historical significance by an expert.

410
00:30:08,881 --> 00:30:10,880
And you have to get a licensed archaeologist.

411
00:30:10,881 --> 00:30:12,880
It's not just some guy off the street with a shovel that does it.

412
00:30:12,881 --> 00:30:14,880
It's somebody who really knows what they're doing.

413
00:30:14,881 --> 00:30:19,880
I guess the most interesting of all the bottles is one that's not just visually interesting,

414
00:30:19,881 --> 00:30:23,880
but to your nose as well, because it's a mustard bottle.

415
00:30:23,881 --> 00:30:27,880
And we're talking about not yesterday's mustard, but mustard that's over 100 years old.

416
00:30:27,881 --> 00:30:34,420
And yet you can still smell traces of mustard as you try and crack the mustard jar.

417
00:30:34,860 --> 00:30:39,100
There are some tools, as you can see in here, but the question is, how did it get to be garbage?

418
00:30:39,240 --> 00:30:42,220
Did some guy say, I've had enough of this and throw it because he bought a brand new one?

419
00:30:42,620 --> 00:30:43,480
Or did he lose it?

420
00:30:43,920 --> 00:30:47,280
I mean, is he still wandering around somewhere saying, where did I put that thing?

421
00:30:47,860 --> 00:30:48,860
And this is a French cannon.

422
00:30:49,380 --> 00:30:50,700
We don't know how it got here.

423
00:30:51,440 --> 00:30:56,420
One extreme, perhaps, is that it came here in an attempt to bolster the armaments that

424
00:30:56,421 --> 00:30:59,020
we had when we were defending against the Americans.

425
00:30:59,021 --> 00:31:01,180
Many people don't realize that during the War of 1812,

426
00:31:01,181 --> 00:31:03,680
Toronto was attacked by the Americans.

427
00:31:03,681 --> 00:31:06,360
And for five or six days, the Americans

428
00:31:06,361 --> 00:31:08,260
were in charge of this community

429
00:31:08,261 --> 00:31:10,460
until they saw the price of gasoline and left.

430
00:31:10,461 --> 00:31:13,480
But this cannon conceivably came here

431
00:31:13,481 --> 00:31:17,080
in order to help us defend against the invasion.

432
00:31:17,081 --> 00:31:20,760
And now it's become part of your experience at Sky Dome.

433
00:31:20,761 --> 00:31:22,360
You just don't come here to watch a ball game

434
00:31:22,361 --> 00:31:23,300
or a football game.

435
00:31:23,301 --> 00:31:25,380
You can actually learn about the history of our community,

436
00:31:25,381 --> 00:31:29,360
which in my estimation is almost as important as winning a World Series.

437
00:31:30,500 --> 00:31:35,140
But for those who are more interested in today's games than yesterday's garbage,

438
00:31:35,960 --> 00:31:39,260
the dome features a stupendous technological miracle.

439
00:31:40,140 --> 00:31:43,420
It's 30 feet high by 110 feet wide.

440
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:45,840
It was the largest screen in the world.

441
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:49,440
The Jumbotron is a large video screen.

442
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:51,780
It's not a solid piece.

443
00:31:51,781 --> 00:31:57,540
The board is actually made up of 67,200 of these things, and these are called trinolites.

444
00:31:58,140 --> 00:32:00,900
And each one of these trinolites contains two picture elements.

445
00:32:02,640 --> 00:32:10,620
And this element are three strips of phosphor, blue, red, and green phosphor.

446
00:32:11,260 --> 00:32:14,800
When you're very close to it, like if you're only three or four feet away from the screen,

447
00:32:14,840 --> 00:32:16,500
it just looks like a bunch of colored dots.

448
00:32:16,780 --> 00:32:21,100
And of course, jumbotrons are hundreds of feet away from the fans.

449
00:32:21,101 --> 00:32:25,540
when you get that far away, you can then see crisp, clean, clear video pictures.

450
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:30,680
And if you take a magnifying glass and look at the television screen at your house,

451
00:32:31,260 --> 00:32:33,800
you'll see these same strips or dots of color.

452
00:32:34,640 --> 00:32:36,620
We can't show live action on the Jumbotron,

453
00:32:36,700 --> 00:32:39,960
and it's not something that's unique to this facility.

454
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:43,780
All Major League Baseball teams have to abide by the rules,

455
00:32:44,300 --> 00:32:47,440
and the rules very clearly stipulate that no video screen

456
00:32:47,441 --> 00:32:50,780
is allowed to show live action while the game is in progress.

457
00:32:51,100 --> 00:32:54,980
If you as a batter are trying to concentrate on the ball coming from the pitcher's hand,

458
00:32:55,440 --> 00:33:00,840
you're distracted by a large video image of yourself in motion above the pitcher's head.

459
00:33:01,120 --> 00:33:04,360
It could distract your concentration on that 90 mile an hour fastball.

460
00:33:04,940 --> 00:33:08,920
Also, they don't want to see that over the shoulder shot from center field,

461
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:11,620
which shows the catcher giving the signs,

462
00:33:12,380 --> 00:33:15,740
because then everyone in the ballpark would know what the next pitcher is going to be.

463
00:33:16,680 --> 00:33:20,520
But Toronto's dome has used its Jumbotron screen for more than just baseball.

464
00:33:21,100 --> 00:33:26,100
There are over 250 events in this building on an annual basis.

465
00:33:26,101 --> 00:33:28,100
We've had auto races in the building.

466
00:33:28,101 --> 00:33:31,100
We've had bike races, dirt shatters, concerts.

467
00:33:31,101 --> 00:33:34,100
Ironically, one of the biggest events at the Toronto Stadium

468
00:33:34,101 --> 00:33:36,100
didn't even happen there.

469
00:33:36,101 --> 00:33:41,100
October 1992, the Toronto Blue Jays capture the World Series

470
00:33:41,101 --> 00:33:44,100
in a triumphant final game in Atlanta.

471
00:33:44,101 --> 00:33:49,100
But in their hometown, nearly 60,000 fans packed the stadium

472
00:33:49,101 --> 00:33:52,340
to watch the win on the giant Jumbotron screen.

473
00:33:55,400 --> 00:33:59,940
It's a surprising twist that a spectacular arena can draw crowds

474
00:33:59,941 --> 00:34:03,900
simply by showcasing a spectacular television set.

475
00:34:05,960 --> 00:34:10,640
But the Toronto Dome discovered that spectacle is a powerful allure,

476
00:34:11,480 --> 00:34:16,320
whether it is a live contest or simply a stupendous presentation.

477
00:34:19,100 --> 00:34:26,100
The ancient Roman Colosseum would fit comfortably within the Rogers Center.

478
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:40,100
It takes about 20 minutes to open the roof once you've pushed the button, but there's

479
00:34:40,101 --> 00:34:43,559
work beforehand before you can get to that point.

480
00:34:43,560 --> 00:34:46,100
You have inspections, all roof areas

481
00:34:46,101 --> 00:34:47,336
have to be inspected.

482
00:34:47,337 --> 00:34:51,340
It takes five people, anywhere from four to six hours, and they look

483
00:34:51,341 --> 00:34:54,774
at every area, every catwalk, every tunnel area.

484
00:34:54,775 --> 00:34:58,280
They're looking for anything that might interrupt

485
00:34:58,281 --> 00:35:03,920
or cause the roof not to function properly. Every time the Rogers Center roof opens,

486
00:35:04,560 --> 00:35:07,140
there is an investment of time and money.

487
00:35:07,141 --> 00:35:10,160
I'd say we're running between $1,000 and $5,000.

488
00:35:10,161 --> 00:35:13,380
You open the roof up for two, three hours.

489
00:35:13,780 --> 00:35:16,420
The sun gets absorbed into the concrete and the seats and everything.

490
00:35:16,500 --> 00:35:17,440
You shut the roof later.

491
00:35:17,720 --> 00:35:21,900
You spend the next eight hours trying to get rid of that heat that you've collected while the roof was open.

492
00:35:22,420 --> 00:35:29,100
The real trick in operating the 10,000 metric ton dome is being able to predict perfect baseball weather.

493
00:35:30,040 --> 00:35:32,500
We're in the central control room for roof operation.

494
00:35:33,240 --> 00:35:39,040
It's in this room we have the equipment that actually monitors and operates the roof,

495
00:35:39,041 --> 00:35:46,540
as well as a radar system that allows us to go online with our weather company,

496
00:35:46,740 --> 00:35:51,180
our weather contractor, and decide whether or not we want to operate.

497
00:35:51,600 --> 00:35:54,180
This particular screen is the Toronto radar,

498
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:56,740
and what you see, the different colours,

499
00:35:57,060 --> 00:36:01,640
is the different amount of precipitation in this actual rain area.

500
00:36:01,641 --> 00:36:04,900
So the darker the colour, the more water you're getting,

501
00:36:04,901 --> 00:36:08,440
and it's giving us a 40% chance of rain

502
00:36:08,441 --> 00:36:11,100
and various other information that we need to know.

503
00:36:11,740 --> 00:36:14,080
Once the decision is made to open the dome,

504
00:36:14,600 --> 00:36:15,880
the process is automatic.

505
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:19,200
Okay, we've finished the inspection on the east and the west sides.

506
00:36:19,340 --> 00:36:21,140
We'll be ready to open in 10 seconds.

507
00:36:21,340 --> 00:36:22,040
Stand by.

508
00:36:27,840 --> 00:36:30,360
Opening the large retractable dome stadium

509
00:36:30,361 --> 00:36:34,880
requires the coordinated effort of 52 separate 100 horsemen.

510
00:36:34,900 --> 00:36:37,900
power engines as panels along tracks.

511
00:37:04,900 --> 00:37:13,140
These are the main indicator lights for when we're operating the roof.

512
00:37:13,141 --> 00:37:19,060
Once we've pushed the open or close buttons, which are here, a series of operations happen

513
00:37:19,061 --> 00:37:22,620
up on the roof and you want to see things happen in a specific order.

514
00:37:22,621 --> 00:37:24,580
That's what these lights let you know.

515
00:37:24,581 --> 00:37:30,360
Up here we have our position, skew and speed indicators.

516
00:37:30,361 --> 00:37:33,600
The speed of the panels is 70 feet per minute,

517
00:37:33,601 --> 00:37:36,760
and skew is the difference in the position on one side

518
00:37:36,761 --> 00:37:38,120
of the panel to the other side.

519
00:37:50,360 --> 00:37:52,000
The retractable roof at Rogers Center

520
00:37:52,001 --> 00:37:55,880
was a sign of things to come in Major League Baseball.

521
00:37:55,881 --> 00:37:58,760
The first team to take the technology to the next level

522
00:37:58,761 --> 00:38:02,060
was the expansion Arizona Diamondbacks in 1998.

523
00:38:03,960 --> 00:38:07,520
Consisting of over 900 tons of structural steel

524
00:38:07,521 --> 00:38:09,880
and using more than four miles of cables

525
00:38:09,881 --> 00:38:11,920
strung through a pulley system,

526
00:38:11,921 --> 00:38:14,180
Chase Field's retractable roof is opened

527
00:38:14,181 --> 00:38:17,240
by a pair of 200 horsepower mollies.

528
00:38:17,241 --> 00:38:19,840
The whole process takes only four minutes.

529
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:23,660
Arizona's success made the city of Seattle

530
00:38:23,661 --> 00:38:26,520
take a second look at its aging dome.

531
00:38:26,521 --> 00:38:29,420
There was this real concern that the Kingdome,

532
00:38:29,421 --> 00:38:31,660
which had only been built in 1976,

533
00:38:31,661 --> 00:38:32,860
was only 20 years old,

534
00:38:32,861 --> 00:38:35,360
that it wasn't really structurally sound.

535
00:38:35,361 --> 00:38:37,460
There were concerns about whether they could build

536
00:38:37,461 --> 00:38:38,640
a new ballpark for the team,

537
00:38:38,641 --> 00:38:40,740
whether the team would have to go to another city.

538
00:38:40,741 --> 00:38:44,440
And in the end, they brought in one of the nicest ballparks

539
00:38:44,441 --> 00:38:46,980
in the major leagues in Safeco Field.

540
00:38:46,981 --> 00:38:48,480
It has the retractable roof,

541
00:38:48,481 --> 00:38:51,540
which allows fans to enjoy the warm summer nights

542
00:38:51,541 --> 00:38:53,480
when the weather is pleasant.

543
00:38:53,481 --> 00:38:55,380
And then if it rains or it gets chilly,

544
00:38:55,381 --> 00:38:58,180
closed the roof.

545
00:38:58,181 --> 00:39:00,480
Even the famed eighth wonder of the world,

546
00:39:00,481 --> 00:39:04,120
Houston's Astrodome, was not immune to the march of time.

547
00:39:04,121 --> 00:39:07,860
When the Astrodome was built in 1965, when opened 1965,

548
00:39:07,861 --> 00:39:09,720
it was very much a futuristic ballpark.

549
00:39:12,660 --> 00:39:16,040
By the end of the century, it was starting to show its age

550
00:39:16,041 --> 00:39:16,940
again.

551
00:39:16,941 --> 00:39:18,540
It was a big, cavernous place.

552
00:39:18,541 --> 00:39:21,200
It was hard to draw fans into the ballpark.

553
00:39:21,201 --> 00:39:22,680
So they went in a different direction

554
00:39:22,681 --> 00:39:25,180
with a new ballpark, which originally called Enron Field

555
00:39:25,181 --> 00:39:26,620
the now Minute Maid Park.

556
00:39:26,621 --> 00:39:29,920
Minute Maid Park, called Enron Field at the time,

557
00:39:29,921 --> 00:39:33,180
was designed to have a retractable roof,

558
00:39:33,181 --> 00:39:36,200
and it made everyone happy.

559
00:39:36,201 --> 00:39:38,900
As more domes are torn down, and retractable roof

560
00:39:38,901 --> 00:39:41,700
technology grows slicker and more efficient,

561
00:39:41,701 --> 00:39:44,600
the days of the dome are numbered.

562
00:39:44,601 --> 00:39:48,140
I think this is the beginning of the end for dome stadiums.

563
00:39:48,141 --> 00:39:51,900
The fixed dome has definitely fallen out of favor.

564
00:39:51,901 --> 00:39:53,980
The retractable roof stadium is what

565
00:39:53,981 --> 00:39:57,860
is going to really be in vogue in cities that have weather

566
00:39:57,861 --> 00:39:58,980
where that makes it necessary.

567
00:39:59,360 --> 00:40:03,000
You should never do a dome baseball stadium unless you have to.

568
00:40:03,740 --> 00:40:07,720
If you're playing in Las Vegas and it's 110 degrees at night,

569
00:40:08,480 --> 00:40:09,560
then you need to do it.

570
00:40:09,561 --> 00:40:15,440
But I hate to lose the fun of the open-air aspect of baseball

571
00:40:15,441 --> 00:40:17,780
because baseball is a game of that nature.

572
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:20,540
While Lou Wolfe and his Oakland A's are content

573
00:40:20,541 --> 00:40:22,920
to keep old-fashioned blue skies overhead,

574
00:40:22,921 --> 00:40:26,820
Instead, they're taking the technology on the ground to a new level.

575
00:40:26,821 --> 00:40:31,640
Teaming up with Cisco Systems, the club is envisioning a stadium of the future that will

576
00:40:31,641 --> 00:40:35,520
truly be a ballpark for the information age.

577
00:40:35,521 --> 00:40:40,440
Cisco Field in the Bay Area, the new stadium for the Oakland A's, is really very much a

578
00:40:40,441 --> 00:40:43,820
21st century example of new ballparks.

579
00:40:43,821 --> 00:40:50,360
They're really incorporating high-tech amenities there, whether it's Wi-Fi throughout the stadium.

580
00:40:50,361 --> 00:40:54,300
Well, Oakland had long wanted its own baseball-only ballpark.

581
00:40:55,120 --> 00:40:59,300
They were able to team up with Cisco to purchase land

582
00:40:59,301 --> 00:41:02,340
and start doing development for a ballpark in Fremont.

583
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:06,400
What that's going to allow them to do is to have a much more intimate park

584
00:41:06,401 --> 00:41:10,280
and one that's going to take advantage of all of the new technologies

585
00:41:10,281 --> 00:41:13,540
that are available in sports facilities.

586
00:41:16,640 --> 00:41:19,980
Fans at Cisco Field won't need to worry about paper tickets.

587
00:41:20,360 --> 00:41:22,360
or even paper money.

588
00:41:22,361 --> 00:41:26,360
Smart devices like cell phones, PDAs, or radio frequency ID cards

589
00:41:26,361 --> 00:41:29,360
will store your seating information

590
00:41:29,361 --> 00:41:32,360
and get you through the turnstiles.

591
00:41:32,361 --> 00:41:36,360
Once inside, ticket holders can move closer to the action

592
00:41:36,361 --> 00:41:38,360
by visiting digital ticket kiosks.

593
00:41:38,361 --> 00:41:40,360
When you walk into a game,

594
00:41:40,361 --> 00:41:45,360
you now have the opportunity to check where's my seat,

595
00:41:45,361 --> 00:41:48,360
can I upgrade that to a better experience?

596
00:41:48,361 --> 00:41:52,080
And if you thought all-you-can-eat seating was an innovation in stadium dining,

597
00:41:52,960 --> 00:41:55,360
Ballpark Eats will also undergo a digital update.

598
00:41:56,260 --> 00:41:59,620
Your smart device will carry a refillable dollar value

599
00:41:59,621 --> 00:42:02,540
and speed you through cashless concession stands.

600
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:05,940
Of course, you'll still have to add that mustard manually.

601
00:42:07,520 --> 00:42:08,920
When you return to your seat,

602
00:42:09,360 --> 00:42:12,460
the same wireless technology will allow you to catch up on the game

603
00:42:12,461 --> 00:42:15,340
as fans who bring Wi-Fi devices to Cisco

604
00:42:15,341 --> 00:42:19,780
will be able to view instant replays from many different camera angles.

605
00:42:29,700 --> 00:42:33,260
We're really trying to help extend the experience,

606
00:42:33,920 --> 00:42:39,080
and I think that's where technology plays such an important role in sports today.

607
00:42:39,920 --> 00:42:44,220
All these new kind of capabilities are going to be standard components

608
00:42:44,221 --> 00:42:48,360
like the retractable roof has become a standard component

609
00:42:48,361 --> 00:42:50,160
in more and more stadiums around the world.

610
00:42:52,920 --> 00:42:55,420
Baseball is basically a traditional game.

611
00:42:56,720 --> 00:42:58,760
Yet the technology that drives it

612
00:42:58,761 --> 00:43:01,980
can make the cutting edge seem archaic in a matter of years.

613
00:43:02,740 --> 00:43:04,920
As the dome stadium fades into history

614
00:43:04,921 --> 00:43:07,580
and the new technology takes its place,

615
00:43:08,260 --> 00:43:11,820
fans continue to enjoy the game they love in a new light.

